Tone of voice
•	Calm and precise, like a good CFO. No panic language, no 'shocking' claims.
•	Respectful to insurers. The site argues for fair, evidence-based settlements, not a fight. Never name or criticise a specific insurer on the website.
•	Short sentences, rupee figures, Indian context (crore, lakh, TPA, PM-JAY, NABH).
•	Show, don't hype. Use real product views and worked examples, clearly labelled as examples.
Accuracy rules for all website copy
These protect our credibility with hospital finance teams, who will check our numbers.
•	Never say hospitals lose the full amount insurers cut. Industry figures for 'disallowed' claims include cuts paid by patients. Always say part of it falls on hospitals.
•	Never promise guaranteed recovery or a fixed % saving. Use "find", "flag", "help recover", "estimate".
•	Label every example and sample screen as illustrative.
•	Every statistic carries a small source line (see Section 6). Consultant estimates must be labelled as estimates.
•	No fake testimonials, logos or hospital names. Use placeholders until pilots agree to be named in writing.
