 3. The insight: where hospitals actually lose insurance money
This is the core story of the website. It must be told accurately, because the simple version ("insurers cut money, hospitals lose it") is wrong in many cases.
The big picture
•	Insurers processed about 3.26 crore health claims in FY 2024-25 and paid out about ₹94,248 crore (NewsBytes, citing IRDAI Annual Report 2024-25).
•	Roughly 58% of those claims were cashless (Ditto, summarising IRDAI Annual Report 2024-25). In a cashless claim the hospital treats first and waits to be paid.
•	In FY 2023-24, of about ₹1.17 lakh crore claimed, insurers paid 71.29%, disallowed ₹15,100 crore (12.9%), repudiated ₹10,937 crore (9.34%), and 6.48% was outstanding at year end (Business Standard, citing IRDAI).
•	Private hospitals handled 6.74 crore of the 11.69 crore PM-JAY admissions recorded by February 2026 (Upstox).
Important: the ₹15,100 crore 'disallowed' figure is not the hospitals' loss. It includes cuts the patient pays at discharge (non-payable items, co-pay, room-rent limits). Use it on the site only as context for the size of the system, with the line: "Part of this falls on patients, part on hospitals."
Who pays each kind of cut (put this table on the Insights page)
Type of cut	Who usually bears it	What DeepClaim does
Non-payable items under the policy (some consumables, admin charges)	Patient, collected at discharge	Shows and explains it; not counted as a hospital loss
Co-pay, room-rent proportionate deduction	Patient, collected at discharge	Shows it; helps staff set expectations before admission
Bill above the tariff agreed with the insurer	Hospital. A network hospital may not collect more than the agreed package rate from the patient (Zurich Kotak claim form)
Checks each bill against the agreed tariff before it goes out
Insurer settles below the final approved amount, weeks after discharge	Hospital, unless its deposit covers it or it recovers from a patient who has left. Hospitals hold 5–10% deposits for this reason (Breach Candy, RF Hospital)
Matches each settlement to its approval; flags short payments; drafts appeals
Deductions on PM-JAY and other government-scheme claims	Hospital. Scheme patients can't be asked to pay (Punekar News)
Tracks deductions by reason; drafts appeals with documents
Claims paid late	Hospital (cash tied up for weeks)	Ageing by payer; daily follow-up list
Cashless refused before discharge	Patient pays, then claims reimbursement	Not a hospital loss; noted for patient experience

Why this is getting harder for hospitals
•	Rates under pressure. Hospitals say package rates have been frozen for years while costs rose (Business Today). Four major insurers suspended cashless services at Max Hospitals over tariff disputes (Angel One).
•	Standardised rates for smaller hospitals. The GI Council's common empanelment platform includes a tariff negotiation step that starts from a base package offer (GI Council).
•	Scheme viability. Over 600 private hospitals have opted out of PM-JAY since 2018, citing delayed dues and low rates (Business Standard).
•	Faster rules, same back office. Since May 2024, insurers must decide cashless requests within one hour and final discharge authorisation within three hours; extra charges from delays beyond three hours fall on the insurer (Business Today). Discharge is faster, but the reconciliation work after it still sits with the hospital.
How much is on the table for one hospital?
There is no reliable public figure for hospital-absorbed cuts at a typical Indian hospital, and the site must not invent one. Instead, the website should let each hospital calculate its own number from its statements (the Claim Gap Calculator in Section 5), and offer a free audit to do it properly.
Consultant benchmarks can be quoted as directional estimates: typical hospitals start at 12–18% claim denials against a 5–8% best-in-class level, collect 82–85% of claimable revenue against 93–96% for the best, and wait 55–90 days for payment against 35–45 days (upGrowth, consultant estimates).

