HEADLINE
The Claim Gap: what India's hospitals absorb, and what they don't have to
INTRO
India's insurers handle crores of health claims a year, and more than half are cashless. Between the bill and the bank credit, money is cut. Some of it is fair. Some is paid by patients. And some quietly becomes the hospital's loss. Here's how to tell them apart.
Sections and charts:
1.	Where the claimed rupee went (FY24). Donut chart: paid 71.29% · disallowed 12.9% · repudiated 9.34% · outstanding 6.48% (IRDAI via Business Standard). Note under chart: "Disallowed amounts include cuts paid by patients."
2.	Claims settled by number, three years. Bar chart: 85.66% (2022-23), 82.46% (2023-24), 87.50% (2024-25) (TaxTMI, quoting the Finance Ministry).
3.	Who pays each cut. The table from Section 3, designed as an interactive list (tap a cut type to see who pays and what to do).
4.	What good looks like. Consultant benchmarks for denials, collection and payment days, clearly labelled as estimates (upGrowth).
5.	The new rules. Timeline: IRDAI master circular (May 2024: 1-hour cashless decision, 3-hour discharge); National Health Claims Exchange live since 2024 (MoHFW); DPDP Rules phase-in from November 2026 to May 2027 (CivilsDaily).
6.	Calculate your own claim gap (below).
Claim Gap Calculator (interactive)
The calculator must use the hospital's own figures and correct mechanics. It must not apply an industry percentage to their revenue.
Input (from the hospital's statements)	Notes
A. Amount claimed from insurers and schemes, for claims fully settled in the last 3 or 6 months (₹)	Exclude claims still pending, so pending amounts aren't counted as cuts
B. Amount actually settled by insurers and schemes for those claims (₹)	
C. Amount collected from patients towards those cuts (co-pay, non-payables, deposits adjusted) (₹)	For scheme claims this is always ₹0
D. Average days from discharge to settlement	
E. Your annual cost of borrowing (%)	Used only for the 'cost of waiting' figure

Output	Formula	How to label it
Cuts your hospital absorbed	A − B − C	"In this period, your hospital absorbed ₹X in claim cuts."
Absorbed share	(A − B − C) ÷ A	"That's Y% of what you claimed."
Annualised	(A − B − C) × 12 ÷ months in period	"About ₹Z a year at this rate."
Recovery scenario	Annualised × slider (10–40%)	"Scenario: if you recovered N% of this, ₹W a year." Never call it a promise
Cost of waiting	(A ÷ months) × (D ÷ 30) × E	"Money tied up in pending claims costs you about ₹V a year in interest."

Worked example to test the build (hypothetical numbers, not a benchmark): A = ₹3 crore over 3 months, B = ₹2.55 crore, C = ₹27 lakh → absorbed = ₹18 lakh (6% of claimed), about ₹72 lakh a year. With D = 75 days and E = 11%: ₹1 crore a month × 2.5 × 11% ≈ ₹27.5 lakh a year cost of waiting.
Below the result: "Want us to calculate this precisely from your statements?" → Book a free claim audit. Results are calculated in the browser; nothing is stored unless the visitor submits the form.
